from common import *
mc('R2.1','sl','Finding the limiting reactant',r'For $\mathrm{2Al+3Cl_2\rightarrow2AlCl_3}$, $0.120\ \mathrm{mol}$ Al reacts with $0.150\ \mathrm{mol}$ Cl₂. What is the maximum amount of AlCl₃?', ['0.100 mol','0.120 mol','0.150 mol','0.270 mol'],0,'The chlorine can react with 0.100 mol Al and form 0.100 mol AlCl₃. Cl₂ is limiting; 0.020 mol Al remains.')
mc('R2.1','sl','Yield after isolation','A synthesis could theoretically form 8.00 g of product. The isolated dry product has mass 5.60 g. What is the percentage yield?', ['40.0%','70.0%','80.0%','143%'],1,'Percentage yield=(actual/theoretical)×100=5.60/8.00×100=70.0%.')
mc('R2.1','sl','Atom economy of hydration',r'Ethene is hydrated by $\mathrm{C_2H_4+H_2O\rightarrow C_2H_5OH}$. What is the theoretical atom economy for ethanol?', ['50%','61%','100%','It depends on the isolated yield'],2,'All reactant atoms enter the desired product, so theoretical atom economy is 100%. An incomplete conversion can lower yield without changing this value.')
mc('R2.1','sl','Volume ratios in combustion',r'$40.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of ethyne reacts completely according to $\mathrm{2C_2H_2+5O_2\rightarrow4CO_2+2H_2O}$. What oxygen volume is consumed at the same temperature and pressure?', ['40.0 cm³','80.0 cm³','100 cm³','200 cm³'],2,'Gas volumes at the same T and p follow mole ratios: V(O₂)=(5/2)×40.0=100 cm³.')
mc('R2.2','sl','Changing surface area','Equal masses of calcium carbonate powder and large chips react separately with excess acid at identical concentration and temperature. Which prediction is correct?', ['Powder reacts faster initially but gives the same final amount of CO₂','Powder gives more CO₂ because it has more surface','Chips always react faster','Powder has a smaller molar mass'],0,'Powder exposes a greater reacting surface, increasing initial rate. Equal amounts of the same limiting solid ultimately yield equal amounts of gas.')
mc('R2.2','sl','Temperature and successful collisions','Why does a modest rise in temperature often produce a large increase in reaction rate?', ['All collisions become successful','The activation energy necessarily increases','The fraction of collisions with energy at least Ea increases','Molecules cease to require an appropriate orientation'],2,'The high-energy fraction increases substantially. Correct orientation remains important, and temperature alone does not change the pathway’s activation energy.')
mc('R2.2','ahl','Order from a controlled comparison','With all other variables fixed, doubling [A] increases the initial rate by a factor of four. What is the order with respect to A?', ['Zero','First','Second','Fourth'],2,'If rate∝[A]ⁿ, then 2ⁿ=4, giving n=2. Order is inferred from the experimental dependence.')
mc('R2.2','ahl','Units of a rate constant',r'A reaction obeys $v=k[\mathrm{X}]^2[\mathrm{Y}]$, with rate in $\mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}\,s^{-1}}$. What are the units of k?', [r'$\mathrm{s^{-1}}$',r'$\mathrm{dm^3\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}$',r'$\mathrm{dm^6\,mol^{-2}\,s^{-1}}$',r'$\mathrm{mol^2\,dm^{-6}\,s^{-1}}$'],2,'Divide rate units by three concentration factors: (mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹)/(mol³ dm⁻⁹)=dm⁶ mol⁻² s⁻¹.')
mc('R2.3','sl','Dynamic equilibrium','Which condition is required for dynamic chemical equilibrium in a closed system at constant temperature?', ['Equal reactant and product concentrations','Equal forward and reverse rates','Complete conversion to products','Zero forward and reverse rates'],1,'Both directions continue at equal rates, so macroscopic composition remains constant. Reactant and product concentrations need not be equal.')
mc('R2.3','sl','A reversed equilibrium',r'For $\mathrm{A(g)\rightleftharpoons2B(g)}$, $K_c=25$. What is $K_c$ for $\mathrm{2B(g)\rightleftharpoons A(g)}$ at the same temperature?', ['25','−25','0.040','5.0'],2,'Reversing the equation inverts the equilibrium expression and constant: K′=1/25=0.040.')
mc('R2.3','sl','Changing only the catalyst','A catalyst is added to a homogeneous reaction mixture already at equilibrium at constant temperature. What changes?', ['K increases','The equilibrium yield necessarily increases','Both opposing rates increase while equilibrium composition stays the same','Only the forward rate increases'],2,'A catalyst speeds both directions and does not change the equilibrium constant or equilibrium composition.')
mc('R2.3','sl','A pressure increase',r'For $\mathrm{2SO_2(g)+O_2(g)\rightleftharpoons2SO_3(g)}$, what is the effect of reducing volume at fixed temperature?', ['Equilibrium shifts to SO₃ and K is unchanged','Equilibrium shifts to SO₃ and K increases','Equilibrium shifts to reactants and K is unchanged','There is no equilibrium shift'],0,'Compression favours the side with fewer gas molecules, here the products. K changes only with temperature.')
mc('R2.3','ahl','Using the reaction quotient',r'At a given temperature $K_c=8.0$ for $\mathrm{X(g)\rightleftharpoons Y(g)}$. A mixture has [X]=0.20 and [Y]=0.60 mol dm⁻³. Which statement is correct?', ['Q=3.0 and the forward reaction is favoured','Q=3.0 and the reverse reaction is favoured','Q=0.33 and the reverse reaction is favoured','Q=8.0 and the mixture is at equilibrium'],0,'Q=[Y]/[X]=3.0<K=8.0. More Y forms as the system moves toward equilibrium.')
mc('R2.3','ahl','Compressing an equilibrium quotient',r'For $\mathrm{2NO_2(g)\rightleftharpoons N_2O_4(g)}$, all concentrations instantly double when the volume is halved. Relative to its value immediately before compression, what is Q immediately after?', ['Doubled','Halved','Unchanged','Quadrupled'],1,'Q=[N₂O₄]/[NO₂]². Doubling each concentration multiplies the numerator by 2 and denominator by 4, so Q is halved.')
wr('R2.1','sl','Yield of a precipitate',r'$25.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.200\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ AgNO₃ is mixed with $40.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.100\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaCl. Use $M(\mathrm{AgCl})=143.3\ \mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$.',[('Write the net ionic equation and identify the limiting ion.',[('A1','Ag⁺(aq)+Cl⁻(aq)→AgCl(s).'),('M1','n(Ag⁺)=0.00500 mol; n(Cl⁻)=0.00400 mol, so Cl⁻ is limiting.')]),('Calculate the theoretical precipitate mass and the percentage yield if 0.516 g is isolated.',[('A1','Theoretical mass=0.00400×143.3=0.5732 g.'),('A1','Yield=0.516/0.5732×100=90.0%.')]),('Explain why weighing the precipitate before it is dry can give an artificially high yield.',[('R1','Retained water contributes to the measured mass but is not AgCl.')])])
wr('R2.1','sl','Purity by back titration',r'A $0.600\ \mathrm{g}$ impure CaCO₃ sample reacts with $25.00\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.500\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ HCl. Excess acid requires $10.00\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of $0.250\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH. The impurity does not react. Use $M(\mathrm{CaCO_3})=100.1\ \mathrm{g\,mol^{-1}}$.',[('Determine the amount of acid consumed by the carbonate.',[('M1','Initial HCl=0.02500×0.500=0.01250 mol; excess HCl=0.01000×0.250=0.002500 mol.'),('A1','HCl consumed=0.01000 mol.')]),('Calculate the percentage purity of the sample.',[('M1','CaCO₃+2HCl→CaCl₂+CO₂+H₂O, so n(CaCO₃)=0.005000 mol.'),('A1','Mass CaCO₃=0.5005 g; purity=0.5005/0.600×100=83.4%.')]),('Explain why incomplete reaction of the carbonate before titration would bias the result.',[('R1','It would leave too much HCl for titration and therefore underestimate acid consumed, carbonate amount and purity.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('R2.1','sl','Two measures of a synthesis',r'Propanoic acid reacts with ethanol to form ethyl propanoate and water. Molar masses are 74.0, 46.0, 102.0 and 18.0 g mol⁻¹ respectively. A synthesis starting from 7.40 g acid and excess ethanol isolates 6.12 g ester.',[('Calculate theoretical ester yield and percentage yield.',[('M1','Acid amount=7.40/74.0=0.100 mol; theoretical ester=0.100×102.0=10.2 g.'),('A1','Percentage yield=6.12/10.2×100=60.0%.')]),('Calculate atom economy for the ester and explain why it differs from percentage yield.',[('A1','Atom economy=102.0/(74.0+46.0)×100=85.0%.'),('R1','Atom economy measures the fraction of reactant mass built into the desired product in the balanced reaction. Yield additionally reflects incomplete conversion and practical losses.')])])
wr('R2.1','sl','A gaseous hydrocarbon analysis',r'Complete combustion of $10.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of a gaseous hydrocarbon consumes $65.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ O₂ and forms $40.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ CO₂. All volumes refer to the same temperature and pressure; water is removed before measuring the CO₂.',[('Determine the molecular formula of the hydrocarbon.',[('M1','For CₓHᵧ, CO₂/hydrocarbon volume ratio gives x=40.0/10.0=4.'),('M1','O₂ ratio=x+y/4=6.5, so 4+y/4=6.5.'),('A1','y=10; formula C₄H₁₀.')]),('Write the balanced combustion equation with integer coefficients and state a limitation of the identification.',[('A1','2C₄H₁₀+13O₂→8CO₂+10H₂O.'),('R1','The data determine the molecular formula but cannot distinguish butane from 2-methylpropane.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('R2.1','sl','A peroxide decomposition',r'$20.0\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ of hydrogen peroxide solution produces $120\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ dry O₂ at conditions where the molar gas volume is $24.0\ \mathrm{dm^3\,mol^{-1}}$. Decomposition is complete: $\mathrm{2H_2O_2\rightarrow2H_2O+O_2}$.',[('Determine the initial H₂O₂ concentration.',[('M1','n(O₂)=0.120/24.0=0.00500 mol.'),('M1','n(H₂O₂)=2×0.00500=0.0100 mol.'),('A1','c=0.0100/0.0200=0.500 mol dm⁻³.')]),('State and explain the bias if some oxygen escapes before collection starts.',[('R1','The collected gas volume is too low, so the calculated peroxide concentration is too low.')])])
wr('R2.2','sl','Gas collection and rate','Constructed gas-volume readings are shown. Reactant quantities are fixed and gas is collected without leakage.'+table(['Time / s','Gas volume / cm³'],[(0,0),(20,18),(40,30),(60,38),(80,42),(100,44)]),[('Calculate the average gas-production rate between 20 s and 60 s.',[('M1','Average rate=(38−18)/(60−20).'),('A1','0.500 cm³ s⁻¹.')]),('Explain the change in gradient and suggest how an initial rate would be determined.',[('R1','The gradient decreases as reactant concentration decreases / fewer reacting particles remain.'),('M1','Draw a tangent to a smooth curve at t=0 and determine its gradient; do not use the whole-run average as the initial rate.')]),('Predict the effect of adding a catalyst on the final gas volume if it does not change the stoichiometry.',[('R1','The same final gas volume is obtained sooner because the starting limiting-reactant amount is unchanged.')])],kind='data-based',figure=plot([('',[(0,0),(20,18),(40,30),(60,38),(80,42),(100,44)],'#186c93')],'Time / s','Gas volume / cm³',ylim=(0,50)))
wr('R2.2','sl','An initial tangent','A tangent to a product-concentration curve at t=0 passes through (0 s, 0.000 mol dm⁻³) and (25.0 s, 0.0600 mol dm⁻³). The reaction is 2A→B, and the plotted product is B.',[('Calculate the initial formation rate of B and disappearance rate of A.',[('M1','d[B]/dt=0.0600/25.0.'),('A1','B forms at 2.40×10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹.'),('A1','A disappears at twice that rate: 4.80×10⁻³ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹.')]),('Explain why the tangent method is preferable to the total concentration change divided by total reaction time for this purpose.',[('R1','The tangent estimates the instantaneous initial slope; a whole-run average includes the later slower reaction.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('R2.2','ahl','An initial-rate matrix','Constructed initial-rate data for A+B→products are given.'+table(['Run','[A] / mol dm⁻³','[B] / mol dm⁻³','Rate / mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹'],[(1,'0.100','0.100','0.00200'),(2,'0.200','0.100','0.00800'),(3,'0.100','0.300','0.00600')]),[('Determine the order with respect to each reactant and write the rate equation.',[('R1','Runs 1 and 2: doubling [A] quadruples rate, so order in A is 2.'),('R1','Runs 1 and 3: tripling [B] triples rate, so order in B is 1.'),('A1','v=k[A]²[B].')]),('Calculate k with units and predict the rate when [A]=0.150 and [B]=0.200 mol dm⁻³.',[('M1','k=0.00200/(0.100²×0.100)=2.00 dm⁶ mol⁻² s⁻¹.'),('A1','v=2.00×0.150²×0.200=0.00900 mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹.')]),('Explain why this rate equation cannot be inferred solely from the overall reaction equation.',[('R1','Overall stoichiometric coefficients do not specify the elementary steps or rate-determining process; orders require experimental or valid mechanistic evidence.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('R2.2','ahl','Zero order in a coloured reagent','An experiment follows a coloured reactant B. At fixed [A], changing initial [B] from 0.0100 to 0.0200 mol dm⁻³ leaves the initial rate at 4.00×10⁻⁵ mol dm⁻³ s⁻¹. Doubling [A] doubles that rate.',[('Deduce the rate law and explain the role of B in it.',[('A1','v=k[A]; first order in A and zero order in B.'),('R1','B can still be consumed in the overall reaction even though its concentration does not affect the rate over this experimental range.')]),('At [A]=0.0800 mol dm⁻³, calculate k and its units.',[('M1','k=v/[A]=(4.00×10⁻⁵)/0.0800.'),('A1','k=5.00×10⁻⁴ s⁻¹.')]),('State why extrapolating zero-order behaviour to [B]=0 is invalid.',[('R1','The empirical relationship has a limited concentration range; if the required reactant is absent, the same reaction cannot continue at that rate.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('R2.2','ahl','Activation energy from a straight line',r'A plot of $\ln k$ against $1/T$ has gradient $-7200\ \mathrm{K}$ and intercept 24.0. Use $R=8.31\ \mathrm{J\,K^{-1}\,mol^{-1}}$.',[('Calculate Ea and the Arrhenius pre-exponential factor A.',[('M1','Gradient=−Ea/R, so Ea=7200×8.31.'),('A1','Ea=59.8 kJ mol⁻¹.'),('A1','ln A=24.0, so A=e²⁴=2.65×10¹⁰ in the same units as k.')]),('Explain why the units of A cannot be given uniquely without the rate-law order.',[('R1','The exponential factor is dimensionless, so A has the units of k; those depend on the overall reaction order.')])])
wr('R2.2','ahl','Two-temperature rate constants',r'A rate constant increases from $1.20\times10^{-3}$ to $4.80\times10^{-3}\ \mathrm{s^{-1}}$ as temperature rises from $300$ to $320\ \mathrm{K}$. Use $R=8.31\ \mathrm{J\,K^{-1}\,mol^{-1}}$ and assume A is unchanged.',[('Calculate the activation energy using the two-temperature Arrhenius relationship.',[('M1',r'$\ln(k_2/k_1)=E_a(1/T_1-1/T_2)/R$.'),('M1','Ea=8.31 ln(4)/(1/300−1/320).'),('A1','Ea=55.3 kJ mol⁻¹.')]),('Explain why doubling the absolute temperature would not simply double k.',[('R1','Temperature occurs in an exponential reciprocal-temperature relationship, not a direct proportionality.')])])
wr('R2.2','ahl','Identifying an intermediate',r'A proposed mechanism is $\mathrm{NO_2+NO_2\rightarrow NO_3+NO}$ (slow), followed by $\mathrm{NO_3+CO\rightarrow NO_2+CO_2}$ (fast). Treat both as elementary steps.',[('Derive the overall equation and identify the intermediate.',[('A1','Overall: NO₂+CO→NO+CO₂.'),('A1','NO₃ is an intermediate: formed in one step and consumed in the next, absent from the overall equation.')]),('Give the predicted rate law and molecularity of the slow step.',[('A1','v=k[NO₂]² under the stated slow-first-step model.'),('A1','The slow elementary step is bimolecular.')]),('Explain why CO can be absent from the predicted rate law even though it is consumed overall.',[('R1','CO participates only in the subsequent fast step in this proposed mechanism; it is not involved in the rate-determining elementary event.')])])
wr('R2.2','ahl','Two barriers in one mechanism','Relative potential energies are: reactants 0, first transition state 45, intermediate −20, second transition state 50, products −60 kJ mol⁻¹.',[('Calculate both forward step activation energies and identify the larger barrier.',[('A1','Step 1: 45−0=45 kJ mol⁻¹.'),('A1','Step 2: 50−(−20)=70 kJ mol⁻¹; the second barrier is larger.')]),('Calculate the overall reaction enthalpy and distinguish an intermediate from a transition state.',[('A1','ΔH=−60 kJ mol⁻¹.'),('R1','An intermediate occupies a local energy minimum and has a finite lifetime; a transition state is an energy maximum along the reaction path.')]),('Explain why the absolute heights of the peaks alone are insufficient for comparing the step barriers.',[('R1','Each activation energy must be measured from the energy of the species immediately preceding that transition state.')])],figure=plot([('',[(0,0),(1,45),(2,-20),(3,50),(4,-60)],'#186c93')],'Reaction coordinate (schematic)','Potential energy / kJ mol⁻¹',ylim=(-80,70),title='Schematic two-step energy levels; connecting lines are guides'))
wr('R2.2','sl','A fair rate comparison','A student compares reaction rates using 1.0 g powdered CaCO₃ with 25 cm³ of 1.0 mol dm⁻³ HCl, and 2.0 g chips with 50 cm³ of 2.0 mol dm⁻³ HCl. The student concludes that particle size alone caused the difference.',[('Identify two confounding variables and describe a fairer comparison.',[('A1','The mass of CaCO₃ and the acid concentration differ; accept acid volume as another confounder.'),('M1','Use equal masses of the same solid and identical acid volume, concentration and starting temperature, changing only particle size.')]),('State a suitable rate measurement and explain why repeats are useful.',[('A1','Measure initial CO₂ volume produced per unit time, or initial mass loss per unit time.'),('R1','Repeats show random variation and allow a mean and spread to be estimated; they do not automatically remove systematic bias.')])],kind='data-based',difficulty='medium')
wr('R2.3','sl','Writing an equilibrium expression',r'For the homogeneous equilibrium $\mathrm{H_2(g)+I_2(g)\rightleftharpoons2HI(g)}$, an equilibrium mixture has [H₂]=0.100, [I₂]=0.200 and [HI]=0.800 mol dm⁻³.',[('Write Kc and calculate its value from the equilibrium concentrations.',[('A1',r'$K_c=[\mathrm{HI}]^2/([\mathrm{H_2}][\mathrm{I_2}])$.'),('A1','Kc=0.800²/(0.100×0.200)=32.0.')]),('Explain what the magnitude says and what it does not say about the reaction.',[('R1','Products are favoured in the equilibrium expression at this temperature.'),('R1','K does not determine how rapidly equilibrium is reached or imply complete conversion.')])],difficulty='medium')
wr('R2.3','sl','An inert gas at constant volume',r'A closed rigid vessel contains $\mathrm{N_2O_4(g)\rightleftharpoons2NO_2(g)}$ at equilibrium. An inert gas is added at constant temperature without changing the volume.',[('Predict the effect on total pressure and on the equilibrium composition.',[('A1','Total pressure increases.'),('A1','The equilibrium amounts of N₂O₄ and NO₂ do not change under the ideal-gas assumption.')]),('Explain why applying “higher pressure favours fewer gas molecules” without considering how pressure is changed is misleading here.',[('R1','The partial pressures/concentrations of the reacting gases are unchanged at fixed volume.'),('R1','Only the inert-gas contribution to total pressure increases, so the reacting system is not driven away from equilibrium.')])])
wr('R2.3','sl','Using colour to infer enthalpy',r'In a sealed tube, brown $\mathrm{NO_2}$ is in equilibrium with nearly colourless $\mathrm{N_2O_4}$: $\mathrm{N_2O_4(g)\rightleftharpoons2NO_2(g)}$. The tube becomes darker when warmed.',[('Deduce the sign of the forward enthalpy change and explain your reasoning.',[('A1','The forward dissociation is endothermic, ΔH>0.'),('R1','Warming favours the heat-absorbing direction and increases the concentration of brown NO₂.')]),('State how Kc changes on warming and describe a control needed when comparing colour intensity.',[('A1','Kc increases.'),('R1','Use the same optical path length and lighting / compare the same sealed tube; colour intensity must not change just because the viewing geometry changes.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('R2.3','ahl','An equilibrium amount calculation',r'A $2.00\ \mathrm{dm^3}$ vessel initially contains $0.800\ \mathrm{mol}$ H₂ and $0.800\ \mathrm{mol}$ I₂, with no HI. At equilibrium for $\mathrm{H_2+I_2\rightleftharpoons2HI}$, it contains $0.600\ \mathrm{mol}$ HI.',[('Determine all equilibrium concentrations.',[('M1','Formation of 0.600 mol HI consumes 0.300 mol each of H₂ and I₂.'),('A1','n(H₂)=n(I₂)=0.500 mol; [H₂]=[I₂]=0.250 mol dm⁻³.'),('A1','[HI]=0.600/2.00=0.300 mol dm⁻³.')]),('Calculate Kc and explain why using initial concentrations in its denominator would be wrong.',[('A1','Kc=0.300²/(0.250²)=1.44.'),('R1','Kc is defined using the equilibrium concentrations of every species, not a mixture of initial and final values.')])])
wr('R2.3','ahl','A small-K approximation',r'For $\mathrm{A(g)\rightleftharpoons B(g)+C(g)}$, $K_c=4.00\times10^{-6}$. Initially [A]=0.200 mol dm⁻³ and [B]=[C]=0. Assume the dissociation is small.',[('Estimate the equilibrium concentrations and check the approximation.',[('M1','Let [B]=[C]=x and [A]=0.200−x. Kc=x²/(0.200−x)≈x²/0.200.'),('A1','x=√(8.00×10⁻⁷)=8.94×10⁻⁴ mol dm⁻³; [A]≈0.1991 mol dm⁻³.'),('R1','x/0.200×100≈0.447%, well below 5%, so neglecting x in the denominator is reasonable.')]),('Predict the effect of compressing the system at constant temperature.',[('R1','Compression favours A, the side with fewer gas particles; Kc remains unchanged.')])])
wr('R2.3','ahl','Perturbing a quotient',r'For $\mathrm{PCl_5(g)\rightleftharpoons PCl_3(g)+Cl_2(g)}$, $K_c=0.0400$ at a specified temperature. A mixture has concentrations 0.200, 0.100 and 0.100 mol dm⁻³ respectively.',[('Calculate Qc and predict the direction of net change.',[('M1','Qc=(0.100×0.100)/0.200=0.0500.'),('R1','Qc>Kc, so the reverse direction is favoured, consuming PCl₃ and Cl₂.')]),('If the mixture were first diluted to twice its volume, calculate Qc immediately after dilution and predict the direction.',[('M1','New concentrations are 0.100, 0.0500 and 0.0500; Qc=0.0250.'),('R1','Qc<Kc, so the forward direction is now favoured.')])])
wr('R2.3','ahl','Temperature, K and ΔG°','Constructed equilibrium constants for a reaction are K=4.00 at 300 K and K=1.50 at 350 K. Use R=8.31 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹.',[('Calculate ΔG° at both temperatures.',[('M1','Use ΔG°=−RT ln K with dimensionless K.'),('A1','At 300 K: −3.46 kJ mol⁻¹; at 350 K: −1.18 kJ mol⁻¹.')]),('Deduce the sign of the forward reaction enthalpy from the change in K and explain.',[('A1','The forward reaction is exothermic.'),('R1','Heating lowers K and favours reactants, so heat is a product of the forward process.')]),('Explain why neither K value specifies the time required to reach equilibrium.',[('R1','Equilibrium constants express thermodynamic composition; the rate depends on activation barriers and kinetic conditions.')])],kind='data-based')
wr('R2.1','sl','Checking a titration result',r'A $25.00\ \mathrm{cm^3}$ monoprotic acid sample is titrated with $0.1000\ \mathrm{mol\,dm^{-3}}$ NaOH. Titres are 24.80, 24.10, 24.15 and 24.10 cm³; the first was a rough trial.',[('Calculate the mean of the three concordant accurate titres and the acid concentration.',[('M1','Mean=(24.10+24.15+24.10)/3=24.1167 cm³; use 24.12 cm³.'),('M1','n(NaOH)=0.1000×0.0241167=0.00241167 mol; monoprotic acid reacts 1:1.'),('A1','c(acid)=0.00241167/0.02500=0.09647 mol dm⁻³; accept 0.0965.')]),('Predict the effect of an air bubble initially present in the burette tip but absent at the endpoint.',[('R1','Some delivered volume fills the tip rather than entering the flask, so the recorded titre is too high.'),('R1','The calculated acid concentration is consequently too high.')])],kind='data-based')
save('R2')
